<BaseLayout
  title="Code search tool benchmark: rg, ast-grep, MCP, and what the data actually says"
  description="331 sessions, 4 production codebases, 15 tables. rg wins 9/12 query-class cells. ast-grep wins one. MCP is faster but costs more tokens. Model routing is the biggest cost driver by far."
>
  <Header slot="header" path={Astro.url.pathname} />

  <article class="post-article">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="post-meta">
        <span class="badge">May 2026</span>
        <span class="badge">Token Economics</span>
        <span class="badge">AI Tooling</span>
      </div>
      <h1>Code search tool benchmark: rg, ast-grep, MCP, and what the data actually says</h1>
      <p class="post-lede">I went in expecting to confirm that ast-grep + MCP + rules was the high-accuracy, low-token path. The benchmark refuted that. Here are 15 tables and what they mean.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="post-body panel">

      <p>The hypothesis I started with: ast-grep with pre-written rules and MCP error semantics produces better search accuracy at lower token cost than ripgrep or the built-in Grep tool. It's a reasonable hypothesis. It's also wrong on the token cost claim, and wrong on accuracy except for one specific query class.</p>

      <p>I ran a controlled benchmark across 331 Claude Code sessions covering four production codebases — two Java services (Spring Boot and Quarkus), a Rails service, and a React/TSX frontend. 97 sessions had active search activity (hit-rate and cost-per-found-block figures). 325 had usage data (token cost and model routing figures). Here is what I found.</p>

      <p>The most actionable finding has nothing to do with rg vs ast-grep: <strong>Opus costs 19× more per session than Haiku.</strong> Model routing is the dominant cost lever. Every search-tool optimisation in this article operates inside a rounding error by comparison. That number is in Table 12 — read it first if you only have time for one table.</p>

      <h2>Setup</h2>

      <p>Four repos, three query classes, two tools (rg and ast-grep with fixed rules), three rounds aggregated. The query classes are:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Symbol definition</strong> — find where a class, method, or identifier is defined</li>
        <li><strong>Exact string</strong> — find a literal string, log message, or config key</li>
        <li><strong>Structural pattern</strong> — find all classes implementing an interface, all components with a given prop shape, all methods matching a call signature</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Hit rate measures whether the tool returns any results at all on first call. Cost-per-found-block (Table 7) accounts for the load tax and reissue cost when zero results force a retry.</p>

      <h2>Table 1 — Hit rate by repo × query class × tool</h2>

      <p class="table-note">% hit on first call, 3 rounds aggregated. Higher = better recall.</p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Repo</th><th>Query class</th><th>rg</th><th>ast-grep</th><th>Winner</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Java service A (Spring Boot)</td><td>Symbol definition</td><td>55.6%</td><td>44.4%</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Java service A (Spring Boot)</td><td>Structural pattern</td><td>10.0%</td><td>0.0%</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Java service A (Spring Boot)</td><td>Exact string</td><td>36.6%</td><td>2.4%</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Java service B (Quarkus)</td><td>Symbol definition</td><td>83.3%</td><td>66.7%</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Java service B (Quarkus)</td><td>Structural pattern</td><td>20.0%</td><td>20.0%</td><td>tie</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Java service B (Quarkus)</td><td>Exact string</td><td>16.7%</td><td>0.0%</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rails service</td><td>Symbol definition</td><td>87.5%</td><td>50.0%</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rails service</td><td>Structural pattern</td><td>33.3%</td><td>33.3%</td><td>tie</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rails service</td><td>Exact string</td><td>27.9%</td><td>4.7%</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr class="highlight-row"><td>React/TSX frontend</td><td>Symbol definition</td><td>63.6%</td><td>50.0%</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>React/TSX frontend</strong></td><td><strong>Structural pattern</strong></td><td><strong>0.0%</strong></td><td><strong>66.7%</strong></td><td><strong>ast-grep</strong></td></tr>
            <tr class="highlight-row"><td>React/TSX frontend</td><td>Exact string</td><td>34.5%</td><td>6.9%</td><td>rg</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p><strong>rg wins 9/12, ties 2, loses 1.</strong> The single ast-grep win is React/TSX structural patterns — the one query class where rg returns 0% and ast-grep returns 66.7%. Across every other query class and every other repo, rg wins outright. This is a decisive result, not a nuanced one.</p>

      <h2>The one case where ast-grep is mandatory</h2>

      <p>The React/TSX structural pattern cell is not close. rg returns zero on every structural query against a JSX/TSX codebase. Not low hit rate — zero. The reason is that JSX component structure, prop shapes, and hook usage patterns require CST-aware matching. A regex over text cannot find "all components that use this hook" reliably when the hook call may be nested, renamed, or composed.</p>

      <p>Two things make ast-grep work here and fail elsewhere:</p>

      <p><strong>1. CST dump before rule authoring.</strong> The hidden node problem: JSX component trees have intermediate wrapper nodes that are not visible from reading the source. A <code>has</code> check fails silently if the target node is nested deeper than expected. Running <code>dump_syntax_tree(format: cst)</code> on a minimal example before writing the rule reveals the actual tree shape. Without it, rules return zero — which looks identical to "not found."</p>

      <p><strong>2. Language flag must match file extension.</strong> Specifying <code>language: tsx</code> on a codebase that is 98% <code>.jsx</code> produces mass-zero results with no parse errors, no warnings. Always verify the file extension distribution first. <code>tsx</code> and <code>jsx</code> are not interchangeable flags.</p>

      <p>And <code>stopBy: end</code> is mandatory at every <code>has</code> level above the target. Without it, ast-grep only checks direct children and misses deeply-nested nodes silently.</p>

      <h2>Table 2 — Latency (CLI vs MCP, wall-clock median ms)</h2>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Repo</th><th>CLI rg p50</th><th>CLI ast-grep p50</th><th>MCP find_code p50</th><th>MCP advantage</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Java service A</td><td>350ms</td><td>313ms</td><td>72ms</td><td>4.4×</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Java service B</td><td>268ms</td><td>257ms</td><td>69ms</td><td>3.7×</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rails service</td><td>2,544ms</td><td>1,916ms</td><td>900ms</td><td>2.1×</td></tr>
            <tr><td>React/TSX frontend</td><td>988ms</td><td>1,128ms</td><td>449ms</td><td>2.2×</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>MCP wins on every repo. 2–5× faster wall-clock, all sub-second. <strong>Latency is the real MCP advantage</strong> — not token cost, not error semantics. The Rails service CLI call at 2.5 seconds is high enough to be session-perceptible; MCP brings it under one second. Use MCP if you care about wall-clock speed. Use Grep or Bash if you care about token cost.</p>

      <h2>Token cost: MCP is not cheaper</h2>

      <p>The hypothesis that MCP + ast-grep would be cheaper on tokens is refuted by the data.</p>

      <h3>Table 3 — Per-call invocation prompt size (tokens)</h3>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Tool</th><th>N</th><th>Mean</th><th>Median</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Grep</td><td>492</td><td>63.8</td><td>65</td></tr>
            <tr><td>MCP find_code</td><td>93</td><td>66.8</td><td>66</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Bash + ast-grep</td><td>280</td><td>85.8</td><td>66</td></tr>
            <tr><td>MCP find_code_by_rule</td><td>18</td><td>109.7</td><td>97</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Bash + rg</td><td>25</td><td>126.0</td><td>113</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Table 4 — Per-call response size (tokens)</h3>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Tool</th><th>N</th><th>Mean</th><th>p90</th><th>p99</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Bash + rg</td><td>25</td><td>43</td><td>89</td><td>101</td></tr>
            <tr><td>MCP find_code</td><td>106</td><td>217</td><td>589</td><td>2,010</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Bash + ast-grep</td><td>299</td><td>232</td><td>543</td><td>4,656</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Grep</td><td>404</td><td>260</td><td>753</td><td>2,488</td></tr>
            <tr><td>MCP find_code_by_rule</td><td>27</td><td>580</td><td>2,951</td><td>4,238</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="table-note">Caveat: Bash+rg sample is Opus-only, mostly <code>rg -l</code> file-listing, not match-with-context. Honest comparison: rg and ast-grep are comparable when used the same way.</p>

      <h3>Table 5 — Round-trip cost per call (invocation + response, tokens)</h3>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Inv</th><th>Resp mean</th><th>Round-trip</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Bash + rg</td><td>126</td><td>43</td><td>169</td></tr>
            <tr><td>MCP find_code</td><td>67</td><td>217</td><td>284</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Bash + ast-grep</td><td>86</td><td>232</td><td>317</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Grep</td><td>64</td><td>260</td><td>324</td></tr>
            <tr><td>MCP find_code_by_rule</td><td>110</td><td>580</td><td>690</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Table 6 — Session-total cost (1,576 MCP load tax + N × round-trip)</h3>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Session size N</th><th>Grep</th><th>Bash</th><th>MCP</th><th>MCP − Grep</th><th>MCP − Bash</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>5 (median)</td><td>1,621</td><td>1,529</td><td>3,407</td><td>+1,787</td><td>+1,878</td></tr>
            <tr><td>11 (p75)</td><td>3,566</td><td>3,365</td><td>5,605</td><td>+2,039</td><td>+2,241</td></tr>
            <tr><td>27 (p90)</td><td>8,753</td><td>8,258</td><td>11,466</td><td>+2,713</td><td>+3,208</td></tr>
            <tr><td>79 (p99)</td><td>25,610</td><td>24,163</td><td>30,513</td><td>+4,903</td><td>+6,350</td></tr>
            <tr><td>200 (hypothetical)</td><td>64,836</td><td>61,173</td><td>74,834</td><td>+9,998</td><td>+13,661</td></tr>
            <tr><td>500 (hypothetical)</td><td>162,091</td><td>152,932</td><td>184,721</td><td>+22,631</td><td>+31,789</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p><strong>MCP never crosses over.</strong> At every session size measured — median (5 calls), p75 (11), p90 (27), p99 (79) — MCP costs more tokens than Grep or Bash. The 1,576-token load tax at session start is never amortized. The MCP round-trip is cheaper per call than Grep (284 vs 324), but not cheap enough to offset the fixed cost. If you are optimising for token cost, Grep or Bash+rg is the right tool. If you are optimising for latency or structured error semantics, MCP is the right tool. These are different optimisation targets.</p>

      <h2>Table 7 — Cost-per-found-block</h2>

      <p class="table-note">iter = (100 / hit%) × (1 + zero% × reissue%). Accounts for the full cost of finding one result, including retries on zero. Lower = better.</p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Repo</th><th>Query class</th><th>rg</th><th>ast-grep</th><th>Winner</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Java service A</td><td>Symbol definition</td><td>377</td><td>885</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Java service A</td><td>Structural pattern</td><td>2,095</td><td>∞</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Java service A</td><td>Exact string</td><td>572</td><td>16,370</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Java service B</td><td>Symbol definition</td><td>251</td><td>589</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Java service B</td><td>Structural pattern</td><td>1,047</td><td>1,964</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Java service B</td><td>Exact string</td><td>1,254</td><td>∞</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rails service</td><td>Symbol definition</td><td>239</td><td>786</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rails service</td><td>Structural pattern</td><td>629</td><td>1,180</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rails service</td><td>Exact string</td><td>751</td><td>8,359</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr><td>React/TSX frontend</td><td>Symbol definition</td><td>329</td><td>786</td><td>rg</td></tr>
            <tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>React/TSX frontend</strong></td><td><strong>Structural pattern</strong></td><td><strong>∞</strong></td><td><strong>589</strong></td><td><strong>ast-grep</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>React/TSX frontend</td><td>Exact string</td><td>607</td><td>5,694</td><td>rg</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>rg wins 9/12 cells. The ∞ values represent 0% hit rate — the tool cannot find the answer at any cost. ast-grep shows ∞ on two Java cells where it returned zero hits across all rounds. rg shows ∞ on JSX structural — the one confirmed ast-grep use case.</p>

      <h2>Table 8 — Zero-result and rabbit-hole behaviour</h2>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Tool</th><th>N calls</th><th>Zero-result %</th><th>Reissue rate on zero</th><th>Effective reissue %</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Bash + rg/ast-grep</td><td>305</td><td>34.1%</td><td>70.2%</td><td>23.9%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Grep</td><td>492</td><td>24.6%</td><td>76.0%</td><td>18.7%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>MCP find_code</td><td>93</td><td>40.9%</td><td>50.0%</td><td>20.5%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>MCP's structured error semantics do reduce the zero-result reissue rate — when a zero result comes back with a structured error, the model recovers more cleanly than when a zero result is an empty string. But the net effect is 4–6 percentage points (MCP: 20.5% effective reissue vs Grep: 18.7%). Small. The zero-result problem is mostly a query-class routing problem, not a tool interface problem. Do not choose MCP for this reason.</p>

      <h2>Model routing: the number that dwarfs all of this</h2>

      <p>All of the above is search-layer optimisation. Table 12 puts it in context.</p>

      <h3>Table 9 — Search volume by model family</h3>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Model</th><th>Sessions</th><th>Total searches</th><th>Mean/session</th><th>p50</th><th>p75</th><th>p90</th><th>Max</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Sonnet</td><td>61</td><td>394</td><td>6.46</td><td>4</td><td>6</td><td>12</td><td>38</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Opus</td><td>38</td><td>467</td><td>12.29</td><td>6</td><td>18</td><td>33</td><td>79</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Haiku</td><td>—</td><td>0</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Opus searches roughly twice per session compared to Sonnet. Haiku does zero searches — it acts exclusively on paths supplied by the orchestrator (Read, Edit, Write, known Bash commands). Haiku is not a search agent; it is a file-operation agent. Routing file ops to Haiku saves money without touching the search layer at all.</p>

      <h3>Table 10 — Scope discipline by model</h3>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Model</th><th>N calls</th><th>Scoped %</th><th>Resp mean</th><th>Files/call mean</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Sonnet</td><td>395</td><td>90.1%</td><td>209</td><td>6.0</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Opus</td><td>467</td><td>90.6%</td><td>284</td><td>9.0</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Scoping discipline is equal — both models scope ~90% of calls. The difference is result size: Opus pulls ~36% larger responses and ~50% more files per call. Opus isn't less disciplined, it's more thorough on the same call.</p>

      <h3>Table 12 — Real per-session cost by model</h3>

      <p class="table-note">From JSONL usage fields. Cache reads excluded (90% discount).</p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Model</th><th>Sessions</th><th>Tokens/session</th><th>Tokens/msg</th><th>$/session (est.)</th><th>Ratio</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Haiku</td><td>7</td><td>492,482</td><td>9,878</td><td>$0.64</td><td>1×</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sonnet</td><td>191</td><td>398,488</td><td>5,286</td><td>$1.87</td><td>2.9×</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Opus</td><td>127</td><td>579,905</td><td>7,684</td><td>$12.41</td><td>19×</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Opus costs 19× more per session than Haiku. Sonnet costs 2.9×. The entire MCP load tax (1,576 tokens) represents 0.27% of an Opus session's token budget. Optimising rg vs ast-grep vs MCP while defaulting to Opus for search-heavy tasks is rearranging deck chairs.</p>

      <p>The practical implication: search-heavy reasoning belongs on Sonnet. Haiku handles all file operations on known paths with zero search overhead. Opus is reserved for architecture, security review, and high-stakes decisions where its reasoning quality justifies the cost.</p>

      <h2>LSP: where it fits</h2>

      <p>Neither rg nor ast-grep handles semantic resolution — inheritance chains, type inference, go-to-definition across file boundaries. That's LSP territory.</p>

      <p>The routing rule: use LSP for <code>goToImplementation</code>, <code>hover</code>, and type resolution. One caveat: LSP with a persistent warm index (like jdtls for Java) is viable in agent flow once the index is warm. LSP without a persistent cache — common for TypeScript — is cold on every session and too slow to use inline. For TypeScript semantic resolution, ast-grep with a structural rule is the practical alternative.</p>

      <h2>Decision matrix</h2>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Need</th><th>Default</th><th>Fallback / condition</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Free-text / exact string / log line</td><td>rg or Grep</td><td>—</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Symbol definition (Java / Ruby / Rails)</td><td>rg</td><td>ast-grep if rg returns zero on a known-shape query</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Symbol def / structural (JSX/TSX)</td><td>ast-grep</td><td>— (rg silently misses)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Annotation-agnostic Java method lookup</td><td>ast-grep <code>kind: method_declaration</code></td><td>—</td></tr>
            <tr><td>goToDefinition / hover / type resolution</td><td>LSP</td><td>Warm index required; cold sessions: use ast-grep</td></tr>
            <tr><td>File path discovery</td><td>Glob</td><td>—</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Act on known path (read/edit/test)</td><td>Haiku</td><td>Haiku does zero searches — supply paths from orchestrator</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Search-heavy reasoning</td><td>Sonnet</td><td>Opus only for high-stakes decisions</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Architecture / security review</td><td>Opus</td><td>—</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>What the benchmark actually says</h2>

      <p>The original hypothesis — ast-grep + MCP + rules is the high-accuracy, low-token path — is wrong on tokens and only partially right on accuracy. rg wins hit rate and cost-per-found-block in 10–11 of 12 cells across four codebases. ast-grep wins one cell decisively: JSX/TSX structural patterns, where rg returns zero.</p>

      <p>MCP is worth using for latency (2–5× faster wall-clock) and for structured error semantics on zero results (4–6pp reduction in rabbit-hole rate). It is not worth using to save tokens — it costs more at every session size measured.</p>

      <p>The largest cost lever in this entire dataset is model selection. Opus costs 19× more per session than Haiku. Routing search-heavy work to Sonnet and file operations to Haiku has more token impact than any search tool choice.</p>

      <p>The practical conclusion is not "use rg" or "use ast-grep." It is: route by query class, verify language flags before writing ast-grep rules, dump the CST before writing any structural rule, and treat zero results from either tool as a routing signal rather than a definitive answer.</p>

      <p class="post-cta">
        The search routing logic, CST dump workflow, and model dispatch rules are part of the
        <a href="/post/claude-code-harness">Claude Code Harness</a>.
      </p>

    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

